<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlos Ibáñez del Campo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlos Ibáñez del Campo was a Chilean army officer who dominated politics after the military interventions of 1924–1925 and ruled as an authoritarian president from 1927 to 1931. His regime built public works but collapsed under the impact of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>. He returned to power by election in 1952 and served as president until 1958.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlos-ibanez-del-campo.jpg" alt="Carlos Ibáñez del Campo" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manuel Herrera · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlos_Ibanez_del_Campo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abroad, Víctor Paz Estenssoro’s Revolutionary Nationalist Movement in Bolivia and <b>Carlos Ibáñez del Campo</b> in Chile identified with Peronism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/peronism">Peronism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>…Evita&quot;), was the official anthem of the Female Peronist Party and has become popular among Peronist feminists, and the &quot;Marcha Perón-Ibáñez&quot;, with words by Alberto Marino, was performed by Héctor Ángel Benedetti in 1953 to mark the friendship of Perón and the Chilean president <b>Carlos Ibáñez del Campo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/peronist-march">Peronist March</a></li></ul></section>
